 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Outputs",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The three outputs are constant-current sinks rated to 12 V, set at 16.5 mA typ. They switch on with staggered delays so that the supply sees less ripple. A precision internal oscillator is built in. Dimming has 256 steps and the scan rate exceeds 2 kHz."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "A built-in regulator lets the chip run from supplies up to 24 V with only a resistor added in series with VDD. Power-on and power-down reset circuits are built in."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Data protocol",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Data is a single-wire NRZ code at 800 kbps; each bit is a high pulse followed by a low, and the length of the high part tells a 0 from a 1 (see the data transfer times). After power-on reset the first chip keeps the first 24 bits it receives in its latch and passes everything after them, regenerated, out of DO, so every chip down the chain sees the stream 24 bits shorter. Because each chip reshapes the signal, distortion does not build up and the number of chips in a chain is limited by transmission speed, not by signal quality."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reset and latching",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Holding DIN low for more than 280 µs is the reset code: all chips then move their received data to the outputs at the same moment and get ready for a new frame. Until the next reset the PWM on OUTR, OUTG and OUTB stays as it was."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Chain length and wiring",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Two chips may be up to 5 m apart without any added circuit. At a 30 fps refresh at least 1024 chips can be cascaded. In a cascade the controller drives DIN of the first chip and each DO drives DIN of the next."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Data word",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Each chip takes 24 bits, 8 per colour. The bit map lists R7 to R0, then G7 to G0, then B7 to B0; the data go out in the order G, R, B, most significant bit first."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Parts around the chip",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "A series resistor below 100 Ω on VDD protects against supply spikes and reverse polarity at 5 V; at 12 V the VDD resistor is 2.7 kΩ and forms the divider for the internal regulator. A 100 nF (104) capacitor bypasses VDD. A 33 Ω resistor in series with DIN or DO reduces reflections and protects against hot plugging. With three LEDs per channel at 12 V, a resistor RR goes in series with OUTR; its value follows the RR equation."
  }
